Where a small group of living (small population) is separated from its main population (such as moving over a stretch of mountains or a river, or if they move to a new island so that they are unable to move back easily) they often find themselves in an environment different from that in which they were before.
This new environment has different resources and different competitors, therefore the new population will need different features or adaptations to be a powerful competitor rather than what they previously needed.
The original population has not changed anything, they still require the same adaptations as before.
Eventually, as the new population begins to adapt to its new environment, it becomes increasingly less similar to the other population.
Eventually, after thousands of years or even millions of years, both populations will seem so different that they will not even be called the same species.
